################ 2) bounded buffer livelock / head starvation ################
// ============================================================================
// Author  : Shreyas S Bagi
// Note    : Developed with AI assistance (IBM Bob).
// Purpose : Created for learning and knowledge purposes only.
// ============================================================================
RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] 
##########################################################################
# BOUNDED BUFFER LIVELOCK DEMO  (model of the full-buffer deadlock)
#
#   q    = pending queue, oldest request first. A request leaves ONLY when it completes.
#   buf  = bounded FIFO of request ids, capacity 4.
#   port = ONE service per cycle (service port). A service pops buf.front().
#
# Start state: requests 4..7 already fill buf, the oldest requests
# 0..3 are still waiting in q. Demand is constant: whenever a request completes, a new
# younger one enters q.
#
# Each cycle every request in q is visited oldest-first, in order:
#   not in buf -> try to insert; if buf is full the insert fails, but the request may
#                 still burn the port to pop the front ('queue-only service');
#   in buf     -> try the port; it completes only if buf.front() is ITS OWN entry.
##########################################################################
RPT_INFO @ 0 [DEMO] 
==========================================================================
 VARIANT: BUGGY (result dropped on queue-only service)
==========================================================================
  Behaviour : the queue-only service pops the front, but the result is thrown away.
  Why bad   : the owner of the popped entry is not marked done and is not in buf, so it must
              be inserted and serviced AGAIN - and the port is burned by the oldest request every
              cycle before the owner gets its turn.
  Expect    : LIVELOCK - everyone keeps trying, the port is busy every cycle, 0 completions.
  narrating the first 6 cycles:
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF] ---- start of cycle: q (oldest first) = [0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7]   buf (front..back) = [4,5,6,7]   occupancy 4/4  <-- FULL
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF] port = FREE (one service allowed this cycle), oldest request = 0
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF]   request 0: not in buf, tries to insert (allowed fill for this request: 4/4)
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF]     -> insert FAILED (buf 4 >= 4): request stays in q and rebids next cycle
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF]     -> port is free, so a queue-only service pops buf front = request 4, buf = [5,6,7]
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF]        BUG: that result is DROPPED. request 4 is no longer in buf but not done either: it must be inserted AND serviced again
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF]   request 1: not in buf, tries to insert (allowed fill for this request: 4/4)
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF]     -> inserted, buf = [5,6,7,1]
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF]     -> port busy this cycle: BUF-STALL, request 1 stays in q
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF]   request 2: not in buf, tries to insert (allowed fill for this request: 4/4)
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF]     -> insert FAILED (buf 4 >= 4): request stays in q and rebids next cycle
RPT_INFO @ 1 [BUF]     -> port already used this cycle, nothing more for this request
